---
// spacing.purr.io — the well-spacing playbook as an essay: the
// wrong-sign trap, the natural experiment that fixes it, the model,
// and the honest end. Numbers come verbatim from reports/.
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>Well Spacing Playbook</title>
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://spacing.purr.io/" />
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="How close is too close for horizontal oil wells? The naive answer has the wrong sign. A machine-learning playbook on public Alberta data, taken exactly as far as public data can go."
    />
  </head>
  <body>
    <nav class="topnav">
      <a class="logo" href="https://purr.io" aria-label="purr.io">
        <img src="/purrio_2026.png" alt="purr.io logo" />
      </a>
      <span class="brand">Well Spacing Playbook</span>
      <a href="https://github.com/rbhughes/well-spacing-playbook">GitHub</a>
      <span class="vintage">Alberta public data &middot; 2025</span>
    </nav>

    <header class="masthead">
      <p class="kicker">A CAUTIONARY TALE, THEN A PLAYBOOK</p>
      <h1>How close is too close?</h1>
      <p class="standfirst">
        Drill two horizontal wells too near each other and they steal
        each other&rsquo;s oil. Everyone knows this. So why does every
        naive analysis of public data conclude that crowded wells
        produce <em>more</em>? This project answers that with a
        measurement &mdash; and then takes machine learning on free
        public data exactly as far as it can go, stopping where it
        provably can&rsquo;t.
      </p>
    </header>

    <article class="body">
      <h2>Three numbers</h2>
      <div class="cards">
        <div class="card">
          <div class="num confound">+1.9</div>
          <p><strong>Between pads</strong> (the naive regression):
          more neighbouring withdrawal correlates with <em>more</em>
          production. Operators drill good rock tightly &mdash; any
          spacing study that ignores this inherits the confound and
          gets the wrong sign.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="num true-sign">&minus;5.3</div>
          <p><strong>Within pads</strong> (rock held fixed): the same
          relationship measured across 4,858 wells on 1,416 multi-well
          pads flips to negative [95% CI &minus;11.4 to &minus;0.5].
          The natural experiment recovers the physics.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="num penalty">+16.7</div>
          <p><strong>Counterfactual penalty</strong>: the model&rsquo;s
          median within-pad crowding cost, in points of peer-median
          pace [p25/p75 +7.3/+31.4]. Economically material,
          directionally right &mdash; and not precise enough to
          prescribe spacing. The report says so plainly.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <figure>
        <svg id="contrast" width="820" height="200" role="img"
          aria-label="Between-pad vs within-pad slope estimates"></svg>
        <figcaption>
          Crowding-vs-production slopes, points of peer-median pace per
          unit of exposure. Both exposure definitions flip sign when
          geology is held fixed; the withdrawal-weighted contrast
          excludes zero. Verbatim from
          <code>reports/within_pad_contrast.md</code>.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>The geometry underneath</h2>
      <p>
        Everything rests on a deterministic 3-D reconstruction of every
        horizontal well in Alberta from public directional surveys:
        <strong>105,724 legs across 65,082 wells</strong>, with
        closest-approach, vertical offset, overlap, and orientation
        computed between wells <em>and between a well&rsquo;s own
        branches</em> &mdash; multilateral self-interference, a setting
        public analyses almost never touch. Every lateral is drawn
        below.
      </p>
      <div id="map" class="map-box" role="application"
        aria-label="All Alberta horizontal well laterals"></div>
      <p class="mapnote">
        Each line is one leg, heel to toe (straightened for display).
        Zoom into the Montney or Clearwater fairways and the pad
        architecture &mdash; parallel fans of sibling laterals &mdash;
        is the between-pad vs within-pad distinction made visible.
        Two-finger scroll passes through; use &#8984;+scroll to zoom.
      </p>

      <h2>What survived the gate</h2>
      <p>
        The model &mdash; two permutation-invariant set encoders (a
        well&rsquo;s own legs; its neighbours) predicting calibrated
        P10/P50/P90 first-year pace &mdash; had to beat plain baselines
        on a temporal holdout opened <em>exactly once</em>, or nothing
        downstream ships. It passed: <strong>P50 MAE 71.6 vs 78.1</strong>
        for the best gradient-boosting baseline, with test calibration
        of 12/53/88 against nominal 10/50/90. And the depletion
        counterfactual behaves like physics, not noise: erase the
        neighbourhood&rsquo;s withdrawal history and the predicted
        penalty grows monotonically with crowding.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <svg id="penalty" width="700" height="220" role="img"
          aria-label="Median context penalty by neighbour count"></svg>
        <figcaption>
          Median context (depletion) penalty on in-domain wells, by
          neighbour count &mdash; from
          <code>reports/counterfactual_summary.md</code>. Monotone in
          crowding, ~zero for isolated wells (a hard physics gate).
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        And what <em>failed</em> is printed, not hidden: the
        bare-proximity probe &mdash; walk a neighbour from 1,500&nbsp;m
        to 100&nbsp;m with no withdrawal history &mdash; moved the
        median prediction barely at all. The model learned
        interference through <em>depletion</em>, the channel the data
        actually identifies, and not through distance alone. The
        evaluation gate records <code>approach&nbsp;FAIL</code> in the
        same table as its passes.
      </p>

      <h2>The playbook, as it was actually built</h2>
      <p>
        The repo is organized as the sequence it was developed in
        &mdash; each phase a runnable stage with its own artifact,
        so a team with proprietary data can replay it with their
        inputs:
      </p>
      <ol class="phases">
        <li><strong>0&ndash;2 · Cohort &amp; geometry.</strong>
          Assemble the well cohort from Petrinex production; rebuild
          every leg in 3-D from AER ST37 surveys.</li>
        <li><strong>3&ndash;5 · Pairing &amp; context.</strong>
          Candidate neighbour pairs, pairwise and intra-well geometry,
          then time-aware context: who was withdrawing what, when.</li>
        <li><strong>6&ndash;7 · Dataset &amp; baselines first.</strong>
          Leakage-proof design (pad-aware splits, temporal holdout),
          and plain baselines before any deep model exists.</li>
        <li><strong>8&ndash;9 · Model &amp; the gate.</strong> Set
          encoders, quantile loss; the one-shot test-set opening with
          physics probes that can veto shipping.</li>
        <li><strong>10&ndash;11 · Counterfactuals &amp; the report.</strong>
          Edit a neighbour set, re-predict, difference; write down
          everything &mdash; including the flattering single-seed
          numbers that shrank under multi-seed averaging.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Where public data ends</h2>
      <p>
        The finish line is drawn honestly: an optimal-spacing
        prescription is <strong>not deliverable from public data</strong>,
        because the three inputs that decide it are censored &mdash;
        pre-window depletion history, frac intensity, and within-pad
        geology. <a
        href="https://github.com/rbhughes/well-spacing-playbook/blob/main/docs/WITH_PROPRIETARY_DATA.md">
        <code>WITH_PROPRIETARY_DATA.md</code></a> maps each gap to the
        data category that closes it. If you hold that data, the
        machinery above is built for you to drop it in.
      </p>
    </article>

    <footer>
      <p>
        Data: AER ST37 directional surveys and Petrinex volumetrics
        (Government of Alberta; re-fetched, never re-hosted).
        Full chain of evidence:
        <a href="https://github.com/rbhughes/well-spacing-playbook/blob/main/reports/interference_report.md">reports/interference_report.md</a>.
        Basemap tiles by <a href="https://openfreemap.org">OpenFreeMap</a>.
        Non-commercial demonstration; derived statistics only.
      </p>
    </footer>

    <script src="../scripts/app.js"></script>
  </body>
</html>

<style is:global>
  :root {
